Given the square matrix \([A]\) to be decomposed into a lower triangular matrix \([L]\) and an upper triangular matrix \([U]\) such that \([A] = [L][U]\): \[[A] =  egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 1 \ 4 &amp; -1 \end{bmatrix}\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1</div>Using Doolittle's algorithm, we assume \([L]\) is a unit lower triangular matrix and \([U]\) is an upper triangular matrix: \[[L] =  egin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 \ l_{21} &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix}, \qquad [U] =  egin{bmatrix} u_{11} &amp; u_{12} \ 0 &amp; u_{22} \end{bmatrix}\] Equating the product \([L][U]\) to matrix \([A]\): \[ egin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 \ l_{21} &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix}  egin{bmatrix} u_{11} &amp; u_{12} \ 0 &amp; u_{22} \end{bmatrix} =  egin{bmatrix} u_{11} &amp; u_{12} \ l_{21}u_{11} &amp; l_{21}u_{12} + u_{22} \end{bmatrix} =  egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 1 \ 4 &amp; -1 \end{bmatrix}\] <ul><li>From the first row elements: \[u_{11} = 2\] \[u_{12} = 1\]</li><li>From the second row elements: \[l_{21}u_{11} = 4 \implies l_{21}(2) = 4 \implies l_{21} = 2\] \[l_{21}u_{12} + u_{22} = -1 \implies (2)(1) + u_{22} = -1 \implies u_{22} = -3\]</li></ul> Substituting these values back into the definitions of \([L]\) and \([U]\) gives: \[[L] =  egin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 \ 2 &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix}, \qquad [U] =  egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 1 \ 0 &amp; -3 \end{bmatrix}\] Looking closely at the available options, none match this standard Doolittle form. This implies that the question considers a unit upper triangular matrix configuration for \([U]\) instead (Crout's algorithm format), or a generic scaling layout. Let us evaluate Option D: \[[L] =  egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 0 \ 4 &amp; -3 \end{bmatrix}, \qquad [U] =  egin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 0.5 \ 0 &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix}\] Let us verify their product: \[[L][U] =  egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 0 \ 4 &amp; -3 \end{bmatrix}  egin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 0.5 \ 0 &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix} =  egin{bmatrix} 2(1) + 0(0) &amp; 2(0.5) + 0(1) \ 4(1) + (-3)(0) &amp; 4(0.5) + (-3)(1) \end{bmatrix} =  egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 1 \ 4 &amp; -1 \end{bmatrix} = [A]\] Since the product matches \([A]\) exactly, Option D provides the correct factorization.</div> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2</div>Alternatively, we can directly verify the options by performing matrix multiplication for each choice: <ul><li><strong>Option A:</strong> \[ egin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 \ 4 &amp; -1 \end{bmatrix}  egin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 1 \ 0 &amp; -2 \end{bmatrix} =  egin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 1 \ 4 &amp; 6 \end{bmatrix} 
eq [A]\]</li><li><strong>Option B:</strong> \[ egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 0 \ 4 &amp; -1 \end{bmatrix}  egin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 1 \ 0 &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix} =  egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 2 \ 4 &amp; 3 \end{bmatrix} 
eq [A]\]</li><li><strong>Option C:</strong> \[ egin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 \ 4 &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix}  egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 1 \ 0 &amp; -1 \end{bmatrix} =  egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 1 \ 8 &amp; 3 \end{bmatrix} 
eq [A]\]</li><li><strong>Option D:</strong> \[ egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 0 \ 4 &amp; -3 \end{bmatrix}  egin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 0.5 \ 0 &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix} =  egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 1 \ 4 &amp; -1 \end{bmatrix} = [A]\]</li></ul></div> \[ oxed{	ext{(D) $ egin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 0 \ 4 &amp; -3 \end{bmatrix}$ and $ egin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 0.5 \ 0 &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix}$}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>LU Decomposition Formats:</strong> LU decomposition is not unique unless specific restrictions are placed on the diagonal elements.</li><li><strong>Doolittle vs. Crout Formats:</strong> Doolittle's algorithm forces the diagonal elements of \(L\) to be \(1\) (\(l_{ii} = 1\)), whereas Crout's algorithm forces the diagonal elements of \(U\) to be \(1\) (\(u_{ii} = 1\)). Option D utilizes Crout's factorization method.</li></ul></div>

We are given the following system of non-linear equations: \[\begin{aligned}
f_1(x_1, x_2) &amp;= 10x_2 \sin x_1 - 0.8 = 0 \\
f_2(x_1, x_2) &amp;= 10x_2^2 - 10x_2 \cos x_1 - 0.6 = 0
\end{aligned}\] The Jacobian matrix \(J\) for a multi-variable system is defined as: \[J = \begin{bmatrix}
\dfrac{\partial f_1}{\partial x_1} &amp; \dfrac{\partial f_1}{\partial x_2} \\ \dfrac{\partial f_2}{\partial x_1} &amp; \dfrac{\partial f_2}{\partial x_2}
\end{bmatrix}\] <strong>Step 1: Compute the partial derivatives symbolically</strong><br/>\[\dfrac{\partial f_1}{\partial x_1} = 10x_2 \cos x_1, \quad \dfrac{\partial f_1}{\partial x_2} = 10 \sin x_1\] \[\dfrac{\partial f_2}{\partial x_1} = -10x_2 (-\sin x_1) = 10x_2 \sin x_1, \quad \dfrac{\partial f_2}{\partial x_2} = 20x_2 - 10 \cos x_1\] <strong>Step 2: Evaluate the partial derivatives at the initial values \((x_1, x_2) = (0.0, 1.0)\)</strong><br/>Substitute \(x_1 = 0\) (so \(\sin 0 = 0, \cos 0 = 1\)) and \(x_2 = 1\): \[\dfrac{\partial f_1}{\partial x_1} = 10(1)(\cos 0) = 10(1)(1) = 10\] \[\dfrac{\partial f_1}{\partial x_2} = 10 \sin 0 = 0\] \[\dfrac{\partial f_2}{\partial x_1} = 10(1)(\sin 0) = 0\] \[\dfrac{\partial f_2}{\partial x_2} = 20(1) - 10 \cos 0 = 20 - 10(1) = 10\] Assembling these components into the matrix layout: \[J = \begin{bmatrix} 10 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 10 \end{bmatrix}\] This matrix corresponds to Option B. \[\boxed{\text{B. } \begin{bmatrix} 10 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 10 \end{bmatrix}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Multivariate Jacobian Matrix</strong>: The Jacobian matrix groups all first-order partial derivatives of a vector-valued function. It acts as the local linear approximation helper for multi-variable Newton-Raphson solvers.</li></ul></div>

Given that the random signal has zero mean, \[\mu=0.\] The mean square value is \[E[X^{2}]=\sigma^{2}.\] For a uniform random variable distributed over the interval \([-a,a]\), \[\sigma^{2}=\frac{a^{2}}{3}.\] The RMS value is \[X_{\mathrm{RMS}}=\sqrt{E[X^{2}]}
=\sqrt{\sigma^{2}}
=\sqrt{\frac{a^{2}}{3}}
=\frac{a}{\sqrt{3}}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }\dfrac{a}{\sqrt{3}}}\]

Let \[F_{1}(s)=\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\}.\] Since \(F_{2}(s)\) is the Laplace transform of the delayed signal \(f(t-\tau)\), \[F_{2}(s)=e^{-s\tau}F_{1}(s).\] Also, \[|F_{1}(s)|^{2}=F_{1}(s)F_{1}^{*}(s).\] Hence, \[G(s)
=\frac{F_{2}(s)\,F_{1}^{*}(s)}{|F_{1}(s)|^{2}}
=\frac{e^{-s\tau}F_{1}(s)F_{1}^{*}(s)}
{F_{1}(s)F_{1}^{*}(s)}
=e^{-s\tau}.\] Using the time-delay property, \[\mathcal{L}^{-1}\{e^{-s\tau}\}
=\delta(t-\tau).\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) An ideal delayed impulse }\delta(t-\tau)}\]

