The steady-state power transfer capability of a transmission line is \[P_{\max}
=
\frac{|E||V|}{X}\] where \(X\) is the effective transfer reactance between the sending and receiving ends. To increase power transfer capability, the effective reactance must be reduced. A series capacitor introduces a capacitive reactance in series with the line, thereby reducing the net line reactance: \[X_{\text{effective}}
=
X_L - X_C\] As the effective reactance decreases, \[P_{\max}
=
\frac{|E||V|}{X_{\text{effective}}}\] increases. Hence, the preferred method for enhancing power transmission capability in long EHV lines is series capacitive compensation. \[\boxed{\text{(C)}}\]

Since all three generators have identical fuel cost characteristics, the economic dispatch is governed only by transmission losses. For a given transmission voltage, \[P_{\text{loss}}\propto I^2R.\] Since the line current is proportional to the transmitted power, \[I\propto P,\] it follows that \[P_{\text{loss}}\propto P^2R.\] Further, the line resistance is directly proportional to the transmission distance, \[R\propto \text{line length}.\] The load centre is located only \(25~\text{km}\) from Generator \(G_1\), whereas it is \(75~\text{km}\) from both \(G_2\) and \(G_3\). Hence, power supplied from \(G_1\) experiences significantly lower transmission losses than power supplied from either \(G_2\) or \(G_3\). Since this is an objective-type question, it is convenient to examine the given schedules. <ul><li>Option (B) supplies only \(60~\text{MW}\) from the nearest generator and shifts more power to the distant generators, thereby increasing transmission losses.</li><li>Options (C) and (D) further reduce the contribution of \(G_1\) while increasing the output of \(G_2\) and \(G_3\), resulting in still higher losses because a larger fraction of power is transmitted over the longer \(75~\text{km}\) lines.</li><li>Option (A) assigns the largest share of the load (\(80~\text{MW}\)) to the nearest generator while keeping the outputs of \(G_2\) and \(G_3\) at only \(20~\text{MW}\) each. This minimizes the amount of power transmitted over the longer lines and therefore gives the minimum transmission loss among the available choices.</li></ul> Also, the output of \(G_1\) is within its maximum generation limit of \(100~\text{MW}\), so the schedule is feasible. Hence, the minimum-loss operating schedule is \[\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{(A)}
P_1 &amp;= 80~\text{MW}+\text{losses},\\
P_2 &amp;= 20~\text{MW},\\
P_3 &amp;= 20~\text{MW}.
\end{aligned}
}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Identical fuel cost characteristics imply that transmission loss is the deciding factor.</li><li>Generators closer to the load centre should supply a larger share of the demand.</li><li>For MCQs, comparing the given options often provides the quickest justification when an exact optimization is unnecessary.</li></ul></div>

For economical transmission, the transmitted power should be of the order of the Surge Impedance Loading. The SIL is given by \[\text{SIL}=\frac{V_{LL}^{2}}{Z_c}\] where the surge impedance of an overhead transmission line is approximately \[Z_c \approx 400\,\Omega\] For a \(132\,\text{kV}\) line, \[\text{SIL}
=
\frac{(132)^2}{400}
=
43.56\ \text{MW}\] For a double-circuit line, \[\text{SIL}
=
2\times 43.56
=
87.12\ \text{MW}\] This is far below the required transfer capability of \(500\) MW. Hence, options (A) and (B) are unsuitable. For a \(400\,\text{kV}\) line, \[\text{SIL}
=
\frac{(400)^2}{400}
=
400\ \text{MW}\] For a \(400\,\text{kV}\) double-circuit line, \[\text{SIL}
=
2\times 400
=
800\ \text{MW}\] Since \(800\) MW exceeds the required transfer of \(500\) MW with adequate margin, the most suitable configuration is the \(400\,\text{kV}\) double-circuit line. \[\boxed{\text{(D)}}\] <img src="img/5d76de85cde09b3d.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,420px)"> <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Surge Impedance Loading (SIL) provides a first estimate of economical power transfer capability.</li><li>SIL is approximately given by \(\text{SIL}=V^2/Z_c\).</li><li>Typical surge impedance of EHV overhead transmission lines is approximately \(400\,\Omega\).</li><li>Large generating stations are generally evacuated through EHV double-circuit transmission lines for reliability and higher transfer capability.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not compare only voltage levels; compare transmission capability using SIL.</li><li>Do not ignore the effect of double-circuit operation.</li><li>Series compensation improves transfer capability but cannot make a \(132\,\text{kV}\) line suitable for evacuating \(500\) MW over \(300\) km.</li></ul></div>

The active power is contributed only by the fundamental components of voltage and current. Since the supply voltage contains only the fundamental component, the active power is \[P=V_{\text{rms}}I_{1,\text{rms}}\cos\phi\] \[P=100\times10\times\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{3}\right)
=500\,\text{W}\] The total RMS current is \[I_{\text{rms}}
=\sqrt{10^{2}+5^{2}+2^{2}}
=\sqrt{129}\,\text{A}\] The RMS value of the supply voltage is \[V_{\text{rms}}=100\,\text{V}\] Hence, the input power factor is \[\text{Power Factor}
=\frac{P}{V_{\text{rms}}I_{\text{rms}}}
=\frac{500}{100\times\sqrt{129}}
=0.44\] \[\boxed{B}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Only the fundamental component of current contributes to active power when the supply voltage is sinusoidal.</li><li>Total RMS current is obtained by the root-sum-square (RSS) of all harmonic RMS components.</li><li>Input power factor is given by \[\text{PF}=\frac{P}{V_{\text{rms}}I_{\text{rms}}}.\]</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not use only the fundamental current while calculating apparent power.</li><li>Harmonic currents increase RMS current but do not contribute to active power.</li><li>Do not add harmonic currents arithmetically; use the RSS method.</li></ul></div>

