We need to find the minimum number of children in a queue containing individuals L, M, and N under specific spatial constraints. To minimize the count, we overlap the positions as much as possible by using a reverse placement order: \([N, M, L]\). Let us analyze the position requirements step-by-step: <ul><li>"There are 5 children between L and M": \(M \ \underbrace{\underline{\hspace{0.6cm}} \ \dots \ \underline{\hspace{0.6cm}}}_{5 \text{ slots}} \ L\)</li><li>"There are 8 children between M and N": Since we are minimizing, we place N to the left of M. To ensure there are 8 slots between M and N, N must sit further left.</li></ul> Let us map out this overlapping configuration systematically: \[N \quad \underbrace{\dots \dots \dots \dots}_{8 \text{ children between N and M}} \quad M \quad \underbrace{\dots \dots \dots}_{5 \text{ children between M and L}} \quad L\] Notice that because L is to the right of M, it sits inside the interval between N and M: <ul><li>The total slots between N and M is 8.</li><li>On the right side of this group, we have M, followed by 5 slots, then L. This accounts for \(5 + 1 \ (\text{for L}) = 6\) positions.</li><li>This leaves \(8 - 6 = 2\) empty slots between N and L.</li></ul> Our internal queue alignment becomes: \[N \quad [2 \text{ slots}] \quad L \quad [5 \text{ slots}] \quad M\] Let us verify the remaining boundary constraints: <ul><li>"There are 3 children ahead of N": Since N is at the front of our group, there are exactly 3 positions ahead of N.</li><li>"There are 21 children behind L": Looking at our map, the children behind L include the 5 slots, person M, and any extra trailing slots. \[\text{Positions behind L already mapped} = 5 \ (\text{slots}) + 1 \ (\text{for M}) = 6 \text{ children}\] Since there must be 21 children behind L, we add trailing slots behind M: \[\text{Extra slots trailing behind M} = 21 - 6 = 15 \text{ slots}\]</li></ul> Now, sum all components from front to back to get the absolute minimum count: \[\begin{aligned}
\text{Total Children} &amp;= (\text{Ahead of N}) + \text{N} + (\text{Between N and L}) + \text{L} + (\text{Between L and M}) + \text{M} + (\text{Behind M}) \\
&amp;= 3 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 5 + 1 + 15 = 28 \text{ children}
\end{aligned}\] \[\boxed{\text{A: 28}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Queue Minimization Principles</strong>: To find the absolute lowest count in spacing puzzles, reverse the default sequence layout of the markers to maximize positional overlaps.</li></ul></div>

We are given the simultaneous logarithmic equation chain: \[\log(P) = \dfrac{1}{2}\log(Q) = \dfrac{1}{3}\log(R)\] <strong>Step 1: Relate the terms to a common constant</strong><br/>Let the entire continuous equation string be equal to an arbitrary scalar constant \(k\): \[\log(P) = k \implies P = 10^k\] \[\dfrac{1}{2}\log(Q) = k \implies \log(Q) = 2k \implies Q = 10^{2k}\] \[\dfrac{1}{3}\log(R) = k \implies \log(R) = 3k \implies R = 10^{3k}\] <strong>Step 2: Evaluate the relationships among options</strong><br/>Let us check the structural relationship given in Option B (\(Q^2 = PR\)): \[\begin{aligned}
\text{Left-Hand Side (LHS): } Q^2 &amp;= (10^{2k})^2 = 10^{4k} \\
\text{Right-Hand Side (RHS): } PR &amp;= 10^k \times 10^{3k} = 10^{k+3k} = 10^{4k}
\end{aligned}\] Since \(\text{LHS} = \text{RHS}\), the equality holds true. \[\boxed{\text{B: } Q^2 = PR}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Logarithmic Power Power rule</strong>: The coefficient preceding a logarithmic expression can be moved inside as a power exponent over its argument: \[k\log(x) = \log(x^k)\] Applying this rule directly here yields \(\log(P) = \log(Q^{1/2}) = \log(R^{1/3})\), which simplifies directly to \(P = Q^{1/2} = R^{1/3}\). Squaring all sides reveals \(P^2 = Q = R^{2/3}\), confirming that \(Q^2 = P \cdot R\).</li></ul></div>

We can track the remaining volume of pure spirit systematically using the standard fluid dilution formula. Let: <ul><li>\(V = 10\text{ litres}\) (Total constant volume of the container)</li><li>\(x = 1\text{ litre}\) (Volume replaced in each independent step)</li><li>\(n = 3\text{ cycles}\) (Total number of times the process is performed)</li></ul> The remaining quantity of the original pure fluid after \(n\) uniform cycles is given by: \[\text{Remaining Spirit} = \text{Initial Spirit} \times \left(1 - \dfrac{x}{V}\right)^n\] Substituting our values into the formula: \[\begin{aligned}
\text{Remaining Spirit} &amp;= 10 \times \left(1 - \dfrac{1}{10}\right)^3 \\[1.5ex]
&amp;= 10 \times \left(\dfrac{9}{10}\right)^3 \\[1.5ex]
&amp;= 10 \times \dfrac{729}{1000} \\[1.5ex]
&amp;= \dfrac{729}{100} = 7.29\text{ litres}
\end{aligned}\] \[\boxed{\text{D: 7.29 litres}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Fluid Dilution Formula</strong>: Repeated uniform replacements drain the concentration of an original pure fluid exponentially at a rate determined by the constant removal ratio: \[\left(1 - \frac{\text{Removed}}{\text{Total}}\right)\]</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not treat this as a simple subtraction problem (e.g., \(10 - 1 - 1 - 1 = 7\)). After the first cycle, you are removing a mixture of spirit and water, not pure spirit.</li></ul></div>

Let the incoming volume of new orders arriving each day be \(r\) units, and let the initial backlog of orders waiting to be shipped be \(B\) units. Let \(c\) represent the shipping capacity of a single truck per day. <strong>Step 1: Formulate linear equations for each shipping plan</strong><br/><ul><li>Plan 1: Using 7 trucks clears all pending and incoming orders by the end of the 4th day: \[B + 4r = 4 \times (7c) \implies B + 4r = 28c \quad \text{--- (1)}\]</li><li>Plan 2: Using 3 trucks clears all pending and incoming orders by the end of the 10th day: \[B + 10r = 10 \times (3c) \implies B + 10r = 30c \quad \text{--- (2)}\]</li></ul> <strong>Step 2: Solve for variables in terms of truck capacity \(c\)</strong><br/>Subtract equation (1) from equation (2) to isolate incoming order rate \(r\): \[(B + 10r) - (B + 4r) = 30c - 28c\] \[6r = 2c \implies r = \dfrac{1}{3}c\] Substitute \(r = \frac{1}{3}c\) back into equation (1) to find initial backlog \(B\): \[B + 4\left(\dfrac{1}{3}c\right) = 28c \implies B = 28c - \dfrac{4}{3}c = \dfrac{80}{3}c\] <strong>Step 3: Calculate the minimum trucks required for a 5-day target</strong><br/>Let \(T\) be the minimum number of trucks required to leave no pending orders at the end of the 5th day: \[B + 5r = 5 \times (Tc)\] Substitute our expressions for \(B\) and \(r\) into this requirement: \[\begin{aligned}
\dfrac{80}{3}c + 5\left(\dfrac{1}{3}c\right) &amp;= 5Tc \\
\dfrac{85}{3}c &amp;= 5Tc \\
5T &amp;= \dfrac{85}{3} \implies T = \dfrac{17}{3} \approx 5.67\text{ trucks}
\end{aligned}\] Since trucks must be whole units, we round up to ensure the backlog is completely cleared by the target deadline. Thus, a minimum of 6 trucks is required. \[\boxed{\text{C: 6}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Dynamic Backlog Systems</strong>: Systems involving an initial inventory backlog alongside continuous incoming accumulation must account for both variables across time.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not round down to 5 trucks based on standard rounding rules. A capacity of 5 trucks would leave a fraction of the backlog uncleared, violating the strict requirement for "no pending orders."</li></ul></div>

Let the original total number of toffees inside the bowl be \(X\). We can track the changes step-by-step using a reverse linear equation path. The remaining balance of toffees left inside the bowl at the end of all actions is given as: \[\text{Final Balance} = 17 \text{ toffees}\] Let us work backwards from this final state: <strong>Step 1: Reverse T's transaction</strong><br/>"T took half of the remainder but returned two back into the bowl." Let \(R_2\) be the number of toffees before T's turn: \[\dfrac{1}{2}R_2 + 2 = 17 \implies \dfrac{1}{2}R_2 = 15 \implies R_2 = 30 \text{ toffees}\] <strong>Step 2: Reverse S's transaction</strong><br/>"S took \(1/4^{\text{th}}\) of what was left but returned three toffees to the bowl." Let \(R_1\) be the number of toffees before S's turn: \[\begin{aligned}
\left(1 - \dfrac{1}{4}\right)R_1 + 3 &amp;= 30 \\
\dfrac{3}{4}R_1 + 3 &amp;= 30 \\
\dfrac{3}{4}R_1 &amp;= 27 \implies R_1 = 27 \times \dfrac{4}{3} = 36 \text{ toffees}
\end{aligned}\] <strong>Step 3: Reverse R's initial transaction (Find \(X\))</strong><br/>"R took \(1/3^{\text{rd}}\) of the toffees, but returned four to the bowl." \[\begin{aligned}
\left(1 - \dfrac{1}{3}\right)X + 4 &amp;= 36 \\
\dfrac{2}{3}X + 4 &amp;= 36 \\
\dfrac{2}{3}X &amp;= 32 \implies X = 32 \times \dfrac{3}{2} = 48 \text{ toffees}
\end{aligned}\] The bowl originally contained exactly 48 toffees. \[\boxed{\text{C: 48}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Backward Induction Models</strong>: Fractional depletion problems that include fixed additions are solved reliably by reversing each sequential layer algebraically: \[\text{Previous} = (\text{Current} - \text{Returned}) \times \dfrac{1}{\text{Fraction Remaining}}\]</li></ul></div>
